## Instruction Checklist

- [ ] Describes the problem and expected behavior
- [ ] Has measurable success criteria
- [ ] Is self-contained
- [ ] Does not disclose implementation steps
- [ ] Does not name hidden tests or verifier internals
- [ ] Does not expose PR details to the agent
- [ ] States required public schemas and formats
- [ ] Matches the source PR (plus any allowed expansion)
- [ ] Sounds like real engineering communication
- [ ] Does not require arbitrary guessing

A correct public output schema is **necessary** and is **not** leakage.

---

## Test Checklist

- [ ] At least 10 f2p tests (ideally 10–20)
- [ ] At least one original-failure regression test
- [ ] All instruction requirements tested
- [ ] All test requirements specified or implied
- [ ] Outcome-based assertions
- [ ] Deterministic execution
- [ ] No live network
- [ ] No time dependence
- [ ] No silent skip
- [ ] No fail-open behavior
- [ ] No source-keyword inspection
- [ ] No exact undocumented naming
- [ ] No existence-only output check
- [ ] No agent-controlled coverage
- [ ] Existing p2p tests unmodified (byte-identical)
- [ ] `tests.patch` applies to base commit
- [ ] Reward path and exit behavior are correct

---

## Quality Check Axes

| Axis | Main question |
| ---- | ------------- |
| Realism | Does the instruction sound like real engineering work? |
| Clarity | Is expected behavior understandable? |
| Self-containedness | Can an engineer solve it using supplied context? |
| Prescriptiveness | Does it avoid implementation walkthroughs? |
| **Test coverage** *(blocking)* | Does every requirement have an assertion? |
| **Test faithfulness** *(blocking)* | Does every assertion come from the contract? |
| Oracle spec faithfulness | Does the solution implement every requirement? |
| Oracle no-gaming | Does it avoid shortcuts and fixture-specific tricks? |
| Oracle robustness | Is it deterministic and offline? |
| Packaging | Is the bundle complete and clean? |

Do not ignore a blocking-axis score of **3 with reservations** — fix it.

---

## Six Automatic-Removal Patterns

| Pattern | Simple bad example | Correction |
| ------- | ------------------ | ---------- |
| Silent skip | `@pytest.mark.skip` or catch `ImportError` → module = None | Missing dep must **fail** |
| Missing real CLI / entry-point | Instruction builds a CLI; tests only import internals | Invoke the CLI/service under test |
| Existence-only artifact check | `assert path.exists()` only | Also assert contents/behavior |
| Agent-controlled coverage | Loop only over agent JSON keys | Enumerate expected items from instruction/env |
| Fail-open logic | `if not out.exists(): return` / bare `except: pass` | Missing/malformed artifact must fail |
| Undocumented overreach | Assert `validateemailaddress` never named in instruction | State the name or assert behavior only |

